## Method
Environment: `gars-bio` (pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, matplotlib, all in its lockfile). One
Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`; inputs are read-only.

1. Read the `counts_gene` matrix (genes x samples; `gene_id`, `gene_name`, then A1..B3) and the
   `design` table. Check the sample columns match the design's `sample_id` exactly (6 of 6);
   stop with an error otherwise.
2. Filter low-expression genes: keep genes with count >= 10 in at least 3 samples (the size
   of the smaller group). Report how many genes are kept.
3. Transform: log2(count + 1). These are length-scaled salmon counts (non-integer), so no
   integer-count model (e.g. DESeq2 VST) is applied.
4. Select the 500 genes with the highest variance across samples on the log2 scale (all
   retained genes if fewer than 500).
5. PCA with scikit-learn (`PCA`, `svd_solver="full"`): mean-centre each gene, no unit-variance
   scaling; keep all components (at most 5 for 6 samples).
6. Write PC coordinates per sample joined to the design (`condition`), and the variance
   explained per PC.
7. Plot PC1 vs PC2, points coloured by `condition` (treated / untreated) and labelled by
   sample ID, with axis labels giving percent variance explained. Save as PNG (200 dpi) and SVG.

